---
/**
 * Bearing — the homepage's institutional statement, and nothing else.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT (Huayin, 2026-08-25, front-gate Unit A).
 * The homepage laboratory retired the Three Questions composition: three short explanations could
 * not carry three foundations before a reader has experienced any of them, and the parks are where
 * those ideas get the room they need. It did NOT retire the identity statement. `First Principles —
 * Analytics` and the three terrain names tell a cold visitor what kind of institution they have
 * reached, which is useful before they understand any of the work, and it is the thing the whole
 * corpus has earned. So this file is the surviving half of Hero.astro:
 *
 *   KEPT     the thesis, the three terrain names, one restrained descriptive line. (The generated
 *            formal specimen was kept here too until 2026-08-27 — see THE FORMAL SPECIMEN IS GONE.)
 *   RETIRED  the falsifiable-commitment block (a claim, better spent where an encounter has paid
 *            for it), the two action links (the encounter is immediately below; a "scroll down"
 *            affordance for content one screen away is furniture), and HeroLattice (small, lovely,
 *            and a compressed argument — `materialized · lawful · both exist by law` — that a cold
 *            reader cannot decode in the corner of a first screen. Kept in the repo for the
 *            encounter layer, where it can be walked into rather than glanced at).
 *
 * EVIDENCE became CERTAINTY (Huayin, 2026-08-27). Not a synonym swap. *Evidence* is a thing you
 * have; *certainty* is a question about what your grounds can bear, and evidence sits inside it as
 * one kind of ground — which is the distinction The Ground for Certainty exists to make. The old
 * word named an input; the ruled word names the territory. /evidence keeps its own thesis, that
 * evidence is a standing acquired through a governed crossing and not a sovereign jurisdiction, and
 * this line does not overrule it.
 *
 * NAME THE TRIAD; DO NOT DIAGRAM IT (the same ruling). Never depicted as equivalent, exhaustive,
 * independently bounded, equally mature, or three symmetrical datumwise theories. This line names
 * them and draws nothing, which is why it survived the composition that did.
 *
 * THE TERRAIN NAMES ARE NOT LINKS. Three big clickable doors would be the old architecture wearing
 * a smaller costume. They are a statement — this is the terrain datumwise works on — and they are
 * meant to grow with the reader: broadly understood on arrival, much better understood after half
 * an hour on the site. That only works if the words do not promise a destination they cannot yet
 * deliver, so they carry no href and no id.
 *
 * SIZE IS A REQUIREMENT, NOT A PREFERENCE. Target ≈340px at 1440×900 so the encounter below
 * visibly enters the first screen. The old hero filled it alone (the commitment label landed around
 * y≈770 of 900). If this grows back into a full-screen hero, the composition has failed and the
 * page is a lobby again.
 *
 * THE FORMAL SPECIMEN IS GONE (Huayin, 2026-08-27, cold-reader cleanup item 1). The bearing used to
 * carry one generated Frame-QL mark beside the thesis — `SELECT revenue AT {store, cal.month}`,
 * labelled "Example address, in the data world". It is REMOVED, not restyled and not respelled: the
 * ruling is that it no longer has a clear homepage job, and that quoting a query language on the
 * front gate prematurely narrows the foundational Data discussion into Frame-QL/formal syntax before
 * a reader has any reason to want a syntax. (Had it been retained it would have been respelled
 * `revenue @ {store, cal.month}` — the theory's naming form — which is recorded here only so the
 * question is not re-litigated from the old spelling.) NOTHING REPLACES IT. Do not fill the column
 * with another technical specimen; the bearing is a statement of terrain and the space is the point.
 *
 * The integrity check that mark carried is NOT lost. scripts/gen_transcript.py still executes
 * HERO_MARK against the shipped package and fails the generator closed if it stops serving, so the
 * "the site cannot quote a retired syntax" guarantee survives the removal of the quotation; what
 * left this file is a rendering, not a gate.
 */
---

<section class="bearing ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="thesis">
  <div class="claim ds-measure-wide">
    <h1 id="thesis">First Principles — <span class="territory-word">Analytics</span></h1>

    {/* A statement of terrain. Not links, not headings, not three teaching units. */}
    <p class="territories">Data · Certainty · Intelligence</p>

    <p class="lede ds-body">
      datumwise develops first-principles foundations, languages, and systems for analytics
      across data, certainty, and intelligence.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The type is Hero's, preserved — only the scale and the furniture changed. `ds-section`
     supplies the page gutter (without it the thesis bleeds into the edge on a phone); the block
     padding it also sets is a full narrative turn, so it is overridden here. */
  .bearing { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 2vw, 3rem) clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 2vw, 3rem); }
  /* One column. The bearing used to be a two-column grid — thesis left, formal specimen right —
     and with the specimen removed (see the header note) a 1.75fr/1fr grid would only reserve empty
     space for something ruled out. The thesis keeps the WIDE measure so it still sets on one line
     at 1440; the lede keeps its own shorter measure below. */

  /* NOT --ds-hero. That token is the hero thesis at up to 6.25rem and it owns a whole screen; a
     bearing that big is the thing this file exists to stop being. */
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.95rem, 1.25rem + 2.4vw, 3.3rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .territory-word { font-style: italic; }

  .territories {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.02rem, 0.95rem + 0.35vw, 1.22rem);
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    max-width: 34rem;
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 0.9rem + 0.2vw, 1.05rem);
  }

</style>
